```java
// Mutable: any caller can change state after construction
class Order {
    String status;
    List<String> items;
    void setStatus(String s) { status = s; }
}
```

```java
// Immutable: fields are final, no setters, a builder assembles the whole object
final class Order {
    private final String status;
    private final List<String> items;

    private Order(Builder b) {
        this.status = b.status;
        this.items = List.copyOf(b.items);
    }

    static class Builder {
        String status;
        List<String> items = new ArrayList<>();
        Builder status(String s) { status = s; return this; }
        Order build() { return new Order(this); }
    }
}
```

The builder replaces incremental setters with one assembly step; once `build()` returns, nothing can change the `Order` again.

```java
final class Order {
    private final String status;
    private final List<String> items;

    Order(String status, List<String> items) {
        this.status = status;
        this.items = List.copyOf(items);
    }

    // "Wither": returns a new instance with one field changed
    Order withStatus(String newStatus) {
        return new Order(newStatus, this.items);
    }
}

Order shipped = pending.withStatus("SHIPPED"); // pending is untouched
```

`withStatus` reads like a setter at the call site but never mutates — `pending` still exists, unchanged, which is exactly what makes it safe to have handed a reference to `pending` to five other places in the code.
